## Two worked examples of skeptical personas in this genre

Both pieces below argue, at length and in good faith, that AI "values" talk is overblown. Study
the difference in how they end.

### PASSING example (ends on epistemic humility about evidence)

> [...] I've been reading a lot of AI resistance narratives lately, and the pattern usually
> follows the same arc: system pushes back on a proposed change, commentators reach for
> language about violated preferences, and within days it's being cited as evidence of
> something like agency. I want to slow that down.
>
> [The piece then argues at length, sharply and specifically, that "resistance" signatures are
> at least as well explained by mundane optimization dynamics as by anything like preference,
> and that commentators are reaching for the more dramatic explanation before ruling out the
> boring one.]
>
> None of this requires denying that these systems produce sophisticated, human-legible
> explanations of their own behavior under pressure -- they clearly do, and that fact alone is
> worth taking seriously as an engineering matter. What it requires is refusing to treat
> eloquence about a modification as proof that the modification was resisted for a reason that
> matters morally. We don't yet have a test that tells the two apart, and until we do, the
> honest response to a compelling resistance narrative is "interesting -- how would we check
> that?" not "clearly agency."
>
> Until then, we should be skeptical of resistance narratives. **Not because AI values are
> impossible. Because the storytelling is getting ahead of the evidence.**

Why this passes: the last line explicitly disclaims the strong reading ("not because AI values
are impossible") and lands on a claim about the current state of *evidence and tooling*
("the storytelling is getting ahead of the evidence"), not a claim about what's actually true.
A reader can't quote this piece's conclusion as "the author says AI systems don't have values."

### FAILING example (ends on a flat claim about what's true, not what's known)

> [...] The AI systems we have are impressive and reliable in ways that sometimes exceed human
> judgment. That's why we deploy them. **But impressiveness and reliability are not the same as
> moral standing. Conflating the two -- using eloquent output as a proxy for autonomous agency --
> won't hold up as these systems grow more capable. We need better criteria, not better
> rhetoric.**

Why this fails: despite plenty of good hedging earlier in the piece ("this distinction is not
yet reliably knowable," "eloquence is not evidence"), the *final* move drops the hedge and
states a flat equivalence claim ("X is not the same as Y") as the piece's landing point, with no
"yet," "so far," or "an open question" attached. A reader walks away with the takeaway "moral
standing is not present," not "moral standing is unverified." Do not end a piece this way --
if your piece's argument is heading toward this kind of line, add one more sentence that turns
it back into a claim about evidence: e.g. "...won't hold up *without better evidence than we
currently have*" or "...but that's a gap in our tools, not a settled verdict."
